CRITERION 5 -- TestHandleSwitchScenario_GenericError SUBSTITUTION.  PASS
====================================================================
Production source, internal/services/retirement/settings.go:1778-1800:
    path, err := sm.scenarioPath(filename); if err != nil { return err }
    if _, err := sm.store.Stat(path); err != nil {
        if os.IsNotExist(err) { return &ScenarioNotFoundError{...} }
        return fmt.Errorf("checking scenario file: %w", err)   <-- generic branch
    }
The new injection is a 261-byte basename ("whatif" + 250*"x" + ".json") that
satisfies scenarioPath's string-shape validation but overruns NAME_MAX (255),
so store.Stat fails ENAMETOOLONG -- not os.IsNotExist -- and control reaches
exactly the `return fmt.Errorf("checking scenario file: %w", err)` line, i.e.
the non-typed/generic error the test name defends. Empirical proof it is not
vacuous: scratch copy .../t3v3/mut-switch replaces that generic branch with
`_ = err` (fall through to success):
    coverage_gaps_test.go:814: status = 200, want 500. body:
    --- FAIL: TestHandleSwitchScenario_GenericError (0.00s)
  So the test kills a mutant of precisely that branch.
NO ASSERTION WEAKENED: diffing the test body against HEAD, the three
assertions are byte-identical --
    w.Code != http.StatusInternalServerError -> t.Fatalf
